title: Writing Journal 3 — How Do You Feel About Writing?
authors: Writing Journal 3 SBA, built in lo-blocks
description: A first-year ENGL 1010 student is in a group chat with three members of the Writers' Circle who are arguing about whether writer's block is real. A peer shares a playful writing-attitudes quiz that plots the student on three attitude continuums, and the student weighs the placement against their own sense of themselves. The student tries a two-minute freewrite as a tool against writer's block, tells the story of a moment that shaped how they feel about writing, and hears what Alma notices in it. The chat closes on the peers' last word about writer's block and opens the Print & Submit page.
~~~~

// HOW THE ACTIVITY PANE IS DRIVEN.
//
// Every screen the conversation opens on the right is written as a TARGET EMBED:
//
//     ::wj2_survey [display=target:wj3_sidebar label="The quiz — part 1"]
//
// which does two things at once. It repoints the wj3_sidebar UseHistory at that
// screen (once, the first time the line is revealed), and it leaves a small
// labelled button sitting in the transcript at exactly the point in the group
// chat where that activity happened. Clicking that button repoints the pane
// there again.
//
// The marker is the reason for the form. A bare `wj3_sidebar <- x` set command
// is invisible: the pane changes, and the record of WHY is only in the
// surrounding dialogue. With a marker, a student scrolling back can see where
// each activity sat in the conversation and reopen it. The activities still
// live in the pane — this is not an inline embed, and the chat column stays a
// conversation. (The one inline `::wj2_placement_input` below, with no
// [display=...], is deliberate: that box belongs IN the talk.)
//
// One set command remains, at the very end: after the closing beat and a pause
// it opens the Print & Submit tab (a Tabs field, which an embed cannot repoint).
//
// Timing note: a set command executes while the reveal walks past it, without
// stopping; an embed is content, so it is revealed as its own beat. The pane
// therefore changes one Continue BEFORE the line that announces it, never
// after — which is the order you want anyway.
//
// The review wrapper (writing_journal3.olx) does not yet carry the
// Flash/FocusAction cue Triggers the original's Circle tab has — cues come at
// the end of the review. When they land they will watch @wj3_sidebar.value,
// and an embed writes the QUALIFIED ref, not the bare name an arrow wrote, so
// comparisons on that value must normalize both sides with DefinitionKey(...).

Earlier in the chat [id=wj3_opening]
-----------------------------------

// Shown as history: the Chat block's history= names this section, so it is on
// screen when the student arrives, not revealed by a click. The welcome embed
// is here for its transcript marker; the pane already shows wj2_welcome as its
// initial screen, so nothing moves.

::wj2_welcome [display=target:wj3_sidebar label="How do you really feel about writing?"]

Lee: ok not to be dramatic but I’ve been staring at this writing prompt for my history class for 40 minutes and I have zero words. writer's block is destroying me rn [face=concerned]

A Group Chat, Blowing Up [id=wj3_chat_start]
--------------------------------------------

Richard: I don't think writer's block is actually a thing. you just probably haven't sorted out what you want to say yet, ijs [face=serious]

Alma: What's YOUR take? is writer's block real thing or not? it's on the right ---> [face=explaining]

::wj3_debate_react [display=target:wj3_sidebar label="What's your take on writer's block?"]
--- wait @wj3_block_take.value ---

// NOTE (instructors): ADD a LOCK to the Continue button until they answer

Lee: nah bro, it's more than me having nothing to say. it's like my brain just refuses to come up with ideas or words.

Lee: I used to think like that. but then I got an essay back in 9th grade covered in red ink, and I swear I have not been able to draft anything without freezing up since. so I don't think it's about not planning or willpower — I think it's about what happened to you before you even sat down to write [face=calm]

Alma: wait, that's actually really relatable. I don't think of myself as "blocked", but I do sometimes have anxiety about how to begin, or fear of writing something wrong [face=concerned]

Lee: huh. I've never thought about writer's block like that. So it can be kind of social, like your feelings and anxiety instead of just no ideas in your head?

So... Is It Real?
-----------------

Alma: figuring out why you are blocked seems like the key to unblocking yourself. ok this is going to sound random, but I found this quiz online that's basically "what's your writing personality" and it actually gets kind of real about this stuff. takes like 5 min, no wrong answers, I'll send the link in a sec [face=smile]

Lee: ok, a quiz that might actually explain my own brain to me? say less, I'm in [face=cheeky]

Richard: lmao fine, I'll bite. still not sure about this writer's block stuff though [face=suspicious]

Take the Quiz
-------------

// The explainer comes BEFORE the quiz: the three lines move as the student
// answers, and the course team wanted them to know what the lines mean while
// that happens. Lee's prediction lands here so the lines can test it.

Alma: ok, quiz time. first, a quick look at what it actually measures. then the questions [face=explaining]

::wj3_dimensions [display=target:wj3_sidebar label="Your relationship with writing on three dimensions"]

Lee: I already know I'm going to be dramatically self-expression [face=cheeky]

Richard: it's less ridiculous than I expected [face=serious]

--- wait @wj3_dimensions_done.value ---

Alma: ok, here it is. one statement at a time. don't overthink it [face=smile]

::wj2_survey [display=target:wj3_sidebar label="The quiz — part 1"]

--- wait @s26.value ---

Richard: fine. I went in skeptical, but I went in [face=suspicious]

Lee: ok this was weirdly kind of fun. some of these were calling me out lol [face=cheeky]


What Makes Good Writing?
------------------------

Alma: ok, part 2. now it's not about how you feel, it's about what you think actually makes writing good [face=explaining]

Richard: finally, a section for people who care about doing it right [face=serious]

::wj3_good_screen [display=target:wj3_sidebar label="What makes a good writer?"]

--- wait @wj3_good_5_grader.submitCount > 0 ---

Lee: of course ranking is your favorite part, Richard [face=cheeky]

Your Results
------------
::wj3_results [display=target:wj3_sidebar label="Three dimensions of writing personalities"]

Lee: mine has been parked on Expression the whole time. called it [face=cheeky]

Alma: so that's where your answers put you. want to hear what I see in them? [face=smile]

--- wait @wj3_results_feedback.value ---

Lee: mine leaned all the way toward "avoidance" and honestly? fair [face=concerned]

Richard: mine said I write to get it correct. shocking, I know [face=serious]

Does It Fit?
------------

// The pane keeps showing wj3_results while this is asked, so the placement the
// question is about stays on screen next to it.

Alma: so — does it actually fit you?

::wj3_fit_input

--- wait @wj3_fit.value ---

Alma: ok, forget what the quiz said for a second. where would you actually put yourself, right now? [face=smile]

::wj3_now_input

--- wait isFilled(@wj3_now_purpose.value) && isFilled(@wj3_now_orientation.value) && isFilled(@wj3_now_experience.value) ---

Alma: and a year from now — where would you like to be? [face=smile]

::wj3_future [display=target:wj3_sidebar label="A year from now"]

--- wait isFilled(@wj3_future_purpose.value) && isFilled(@wj3_future_orientation.value) && isFilled(@wj3_future_experience.value) && wordcount(@wj3_future_purpose_note.value) >= 10 && wordcount(@wj3_future_orientation_note.value) >= 10 && wordcount(@wj3_future_experience_note.value) >= 10 ---

Lee: That's really interesting. I'm thinking a lot about myself as a writer and how my relationship is connected to all of my experiences. Like this time in sixth grade my teacher told our class that some people are just born writers and some aren't, and I remember thinking, well, I know which one I am. Ever since then, every time I sit down to write, I hear that line in my head before I've written a single word. It's not that I don't have ideas. It's that I freeze before I let myself try.

Richard: Yeah, our relationship with writing does seem to be shaped by our habits and experiences. Mine is so different from yours because I used to keep a notebook where I'd write one paragraph every single day about whatever happened, even boring stuff, just because my older cousin did it, and I thought it was cool. I never waited for inspiration. That's why I don't think writing has to be this big emotional struggle. For me it’s just a habit, like brushing my teeth. I'm starting to realize, though, that not everyone's habit started the same way mine did

Alma: Growing up, I spoke Spanish at home and English at school, so for me writing was always tangled up with navigating two languages and figuring out where I fit. In ninth grade I turned in an essay I was actually proud of, and it came back covered edge to edge in red pen with comments like “awkward” and “huh?” next to sentences I'd rewritten five times. I didn't write anything I wasn't required to for almost two years. What finally changed things wasn't a teacher telling me I was good.  it was a friend saying they liked my wording in a tik-tok I made about being bilingual and how that means I have sort of a different personality when I speak different languages.

Lee: Can you send me a link to that video? That sounds really cool.

Alma: Yeah, later. For now let's stay locked in.

Lee: ok real talk though. you all just wrote whole stories and I'm looking at the blank box again. this is the exact thing I was talking about at the start [face=concerned]

Richard: Hey, what about you? Tell us a story of a moment, an experience, or a piece of feedback about writing that made you feel like you were a good or a bad writer. What happened? Tell it like a story, not a summary. Make sure to share the way you felt in the moment and how that moment has influenced your feelings about writing. Don't be lazy. Write at least as much as we did

Alma: wait. before you write a word of that. this is where I tell you the one trick that actually got me writing again. it's called freewriting. two minutes. you don't stop, you don't fix anything, you don't even read it back until the time's up [face=explaining]

Richard: two minutes of typing garbage. how is that writing [face=suspicious]

Alma: that's the point. the guy who made it famous, Peter Elbow, couldn't write his essays at Oxford and dropped out of his PhD at Harvard over it. he figured out the fixing part of his brain was strangling the writing part, so he just didn't let it in for a few minutes. he wrote a whole book about it [face=smile]

Lee: ok that is the most relatable thing I have ever heard about a professor [face=cheeky]

Alma: everyone does this one. two minutes, then the story [face=smile]

::wj3_warmup [display=target:wj3_sidebar label="Two-minute freewrite"]

// Required: the chat waits for the timer, not for words, so a student who
// froze for two minutes still moves on to the story with an empty page and
// no penalty. What they wrote is shown on the next screen.
--- wait @wj2_freewrite_timer.expired ---

Lee: ok that was weirdly freeing. I have like 80 words of chaos and two sentences I actually want to keep [face=smileBig]

Alma: ok. let's see what you wrote. no judgment. now make it the story [face=smile]

::wj3_write [display=target:wj3_sidebar label="Your story"]

--- wait wordcount(@wj3_story_text.value) >= 150 ---

Alma: Thanks for sharing your story!

// NOTE (instructors): Also, Katie is happy that this part caught her flaking on the writing task xD

::wj3_alma_response [display=target:wj3_sidebar label="What Alma hears in your story"]

--- wait @wj3_response_feedback.value ---

Richard: ok. I'm still not sure writer's block is "real". but I get why it feels real now [face=calm]

Lee: progress, Richard [face=cheeky]

Alma: that's the journal. thank you for doing this with us, and for the story [face=smile]

// The closing beat is read first; the pause holds the tab switch until the
// student clicks Continue past it. This is a Tabs field, not the pane, so an
// embed cannot do it; the value is coerced by the field's schema.
--- pause ---

wj3_sba_tabs.activeTab <- 3
